<p>
  A standard multimeter reads resistance down to about 0.1 Ω with any real confidence — below that, the resistance of its own test leads and probe contacts swamps the reading. But some of the most important resistance measurements in electrical maintenance — circuit breaker contact resistance, transformer winding resistance, busbar joint resistance, welded or bolted cable joints — fall in the micro-ohm to low milliohm range, far below what a multimeter can resolve. A micro-ohm meter (also called a low-resistance ohmmeter or DLRO) is built specifically for this range. This guide explains how it works, what it's used for, and what to look for when choosing one.
</p>

<h2 id="why-multimeters-fail">Why a Multimeter Can't Measure Micro-Ohms</h2>

<p>
  A multimeter's resistance function uses a 2-wire (2-terminal) connection — the same pair of leads both carries the test current and senses the resulting voltage. At readings above a few ohms, the few tens of milliohms of resistance in the leads and probe-to-contact junction is negligible. But when the resistance being measured is itself only a few hundred micro-ohms, that same lead and contact resistance can be 10 to 100 times larger than the actual reading — the multimeter is effectively measuring its own leads, not the joint or winding under test.
</p>

<h2 id="kelvin-method">The 4-Terminal (Kelvin) Method</h2>

<p>
  A micro-ohm meter solves this with a 4-terminal Kelvin connection: two separate current leads (C1, C2) inject the test current through the component, and two separate potential (voltage-sensing) leads (P1, P2), placed inside the current leads, measure the voltage drop directly across the component only. Because almost no current flows through the potential leads, their resistance and contact quality don't affect the reading — the instrument measures only the resistance of the material between the P1 and P2 contact points.
</p>

<Callout type="info" title="Why this matters for repeatability">
  With a 2-wire measurement, moving the probes slightly or changing contact pressure changes the reading — because you're partly measuring contact resistance. With a proper 4-wire Kelvin connection, the same joint reads the same value test after test, which is what makes trend comparison over years of maintenance records meaningful.
</Callout>

<h2 id="test-current">Why Test Current Matters</h2>

<p>
  Micro-ohm meters inject a much higher test current than a multimeter — often 1 A or more on the lowest ranges — for two reasons. First, a larger current produces a larger, more easily resolved voltage drop across a very small resistance (Ohm's Law: V = I × R), improving measurement resolution. Second, a higher test current breaks through thin oxide films on metal contact surfaces that would otherwise show as false high resistance at very low test currents — the same reason a genuinely loose or corroded joint sometimes reads deceptively "fine" on a low-current instrument. This is also why winding resistance test currents are often specified as a percentage of the equipment's rated current: a low test current can pass through non-conductive paths that would carry no current in service, masking a real fault.
</p>

