<h2 id="choosing">What to Look For When Choosing One</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Range and resolution</strong>: Confirm the lowest range resolves the readings you actually need — joint and contact resistance work is often in the low milliohm to micro-ohm range, while cable and winding checks can extend into ohms or kilohms. A wide multi-range instrument (µΩ through kΩ) covers both without needing a second meter.</li>
  <li><strong>Genuine 4-terminal Kelvin leads</strong>: Confirm the instrument (and the test leads supplied with it) are true 4-wire, not a 2-wire meter with "low resistance" marketing. Kelvin clip or Kelvin probe leads have visibly separate current and potential contact points.</li>
  <li><strong>Test current level</strong>: Higher test current on the lowest ranges gives a more representative reading of contacts and joints under real load, and is less prone to oxide-film error.</li>
  <li><strong>Accuracy class</strong>: For maintenance trending (comparing this year's reading to last year's), consistency between tests matters as much as absolute accuracy — but for acceptance testing against a manufacturer's specified value, a tighter accuracy class (e.g., ±0.5% or better) is important.</li>
</ul>

